Z||000000||Coop88||06-28-2001||07:53 AM||||Just a quickie--how do you get rid of the noise the modem(?) makes when it's dialing up AOL? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||84Bill||06-28-2001||08:31 AM||||Open Hyper terminal and Click on NEW<BR>Type NEW for the connection name.<BR>do a DIRECT COM X (X=the com port number of the modem usually 2)<P>Type <P>AT M0<P>then close the session now the modem speaker is off||||reg||1
Z||000002||84Bill||06-28-2001||08:36 AM||||I almost forgot but some software will reset the M0 register. If this is the case look into the settings and see if there is a "Modem Strings" option. If there is add M0 to the end of the string.<BR>Here is a link that may help. Most modems are Hayes compatible. <A HREF="http://www.computercraft.com/docs/hayescom.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.computercraft.com/docs/hayescom.html</A> <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000003||Formula88||06-28-2001||09:46 AM||||On Windows 9x, open Dial-up Networking, right click on your connection icon, click on properties.  At the bottom of the windows where it says "Connect using" click on the "Configure" button.  There's a slider on this window labeled "Speaker volume".  You get the idea.  Slide it to off.  Some will let you adjust the volume up and down - others just on and off.  Some may have the slider greyed out, so it's not adjustable from the software in DUN.||||reg||4
Z||000004||84Bill||06-28-2001||10:28 AM||||This may help too   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <A HREF="http://www.satirewire.com/features/siliconpines/acf.shtml" TARGET=_blank>http://www.satirewire.com/features/siliconpines/acf.shtml</A> <p>[This message has been edited by 84Bill (edited 06-28-2001).]||||reg||12
Z||000005||87 Pegasus||06-28-2001||11:16 AM||||AOL has it's own modem volume...  you have to go to Set up before you sign in...  and go to modems...  they have three four setting...  off, low, normal, and high..  set it to off and that will shut the sound off.<P>------------------<BR>Friends don't let friends drive Corvettes!!||||reg||3
Z||000006||Tweek||06-28-2001||06:48 PM||||if you connect to aol thru cable/dsl/sat/t1 then its quiet..   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Coop88||06-28-2001||09:18 PM||||None of the above has worked. Any other ideas? Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierospeeder||06-29-2001||01:40 AM||||I have tried everything also, except the network thing.  Aol will always have that modem noise.<P>My g/fs sister has something with their computer that doesn't get the noise??<P><BR>I was thinking to see if aol has the correct modem listed in the list.||||reg||1
Z||000009||FieroSTETZ||06-30-2001||09:31 AM||||open your case and shoot crazy glue into the speaker||||reg||1
Z||000010||baldlobo||06-30-2001||01:04 PM||||first do you know how to use a soddering iron; if yes then turn off computer, open case, pull modem out, find speaker, look on other side of the board, there should be two sodder points under it; put in vice, grab soddering iron and sodder sucker, let iron get hot(as in melting sodder hot),then once hot stick it on the sodder point, when melted press the button on the sucker to suck up the sodder, then repeat on the other point.<BR>after that grab a pair of vice grips and pull, make sure the speaker is facing you.<P>disclamer: do the at own risk. I'm not liable for any damages done to computer or self by doing what is suggested in this post.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Formula88||06-30-2001||01:19 PM||||Or, just put some electrical tape over the speaker.  You'll still be able to hear it,but it'll be much quieter, and it's easily reversible.||||reg||1
